      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The Polar Express (2004) marked a pivotal moment in motion capture animation, though its ambitious technology created what many call the \"uncanny valley\" effect. Director Robert Zemeckis, fresh from pushing CGI boundaries with <a href=\"/movie/8358\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"8358\">Cast Away</a> (2000), attempted to translate Chris Van Allsburg's beloved children's book into a holiday spectacle. Tom Hanks performed multiple roles through performance capture, following the path <a href=\"/movie/2114\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"2114\">Final Fantasy: The Spirits Within</a> (2001) started but with far more sophisticated technology. The film's $165 million budget produced a visual style that, while groundbreaking, left audiences somewhat unsettled by the almost-but-not-quite-human characters."
      },
      {
