      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The film's influence on holiday cinema can't be overstated. While <a href=\"/movie/850\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"850\">A Christmas Story</a> (1983) and <a href=\"/movie/11881\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"11881\">Miracle on 34th Street</a> (1947) grounded their Christmas magic in reality, The Polar Express fully embraced fantasy spectacle. Its success paved the way for more technically ambitious holiday films like <a href=\"/movie/81188\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"81188\">Rise of the Guardians</a> (2012) and <a href=\"/movie/51052\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"51052\">Arthur Christmas</a> (2011). The movie's signature hot chocolate sequence inspired countless holiday marketing campaigns and became as iconic as the leg lamp from <a href=\"/movie/850\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"850\">A Christmas Story</a>."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "From a technical perspective, The Polar Express shares DNA with later motion capture achievements like <a href=\"/movie/19995\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"19995\">Avatar</a> (2009) and <a href=\"/movie/119450\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"119450\">Dawn of the Planet of the Apes</a> (2014). While those films solved the uncanny valley problem by focusing on non-human characters, Zemeckis continued pursuing human motion capture with <a href=\"/movie/2310\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"2310\">Beowulf</a> (2007) and <a href=\"/movie/17979\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"17979\">A Christmas Carol</a> (2009). These films refined the technology but still struggled with truly convincing human characters. SUBHEAD: The Evolution of Christmas Fantasy Cinema"
      },
      {
        "type": "movies",
